Source package: firefox, from the Mozilla Team PPA (https://launchpad.net/~mozillateam/+archive/ubuntu/ppa)

Ubuntu's own firefox package only installs the snap, and Mozilla's APT repository publishes no source (requested in
https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2087959), so Firefox comes from the Mozilla Team PPA, run by Ubuntu's
Mozilla team, which builds Mozilla's release source with Ubuntu's packaging. lockdown-apt-sources.sh configures the
PPA with its source enabled (sources.list.d/ of this repository) and pins firefox to it, below the local
repository, so APT installs the rebuilt package. source-from makes this script rebuild the PPA's source rather than
Ubuntu's.

The PPA's packaging differs from Mozilla's own build mainly in that it disables the wasm sandboxed libraries,
which wasm-sandboxed-libraries.patch turns back on, in shipping an AppArmor profile, which
drop-apparmor-profile.patch removes, and in building without PGO and with Ubuntu's compilers.
The remaining differences, partner and distribution settings and add-on sideloading for its language packs, are
left as they are.

Building
    Launchpad builds the PPA against two more PPAs, as the override-sources-list line at the top of its build
    logs shows, and extra-repositories adds them to the build chroot the same way:

      ricotz/mozilla           an older cdbs, 0.4.182+really0.4.181~mt1, which still has the makefile class the
                               packaging includes. Ubuntu's cdbs 0.4.182 dropped it, and the packaging includes it
                               with -include, so without it the build silently skips compiling Firefox at all.
      rust-toolchain/staging   Rust 1.97, the first alternative of the packaging's Rust build dependency. Ubuntu
                               26.04 has 1.93.

    Trusting them adds nothing beyond trusting the PPA's own binaries, which are built with them. The keys are
    the PPAs' signing keys as Launchpad lists them (fingerprints 9BE528DCBA6F4EAA89326FFBB998E53B7BCC7DB8 and
    AF2410F8B34671DD8C331787A1FBDCECDC193CE1). The lines name the release, resolute, so they have to be updated
    on a release upgrade.

    A build produces 99 packages of about 900 MiB together, mostly the debug symbols in firefox-dbg and
    firefox-mozsymbols, and the language packs. All of them are published to the local repository, which keeps
    only the latest build of each.

wayland-session-restore-new-ids.patch
wayland-session-restore-show-waiting-windows.patch
wayland-session-restore-on-gnome.patch
    Make Firefox restore its windows to their workspaces under GNOME on Wayland. Firefox implements the
    xdg-session-management protocol, but disables it on GNOME (Mozilla bug 2070815) because of Mozilla bug
    2064100: when a previous session is restored from the History menu or a closed window is reopened, Firefox
    adds the shown window to the session under its ID from the previous session, which mutter rejects with a
    name_in_use protocol error, as the protocol requires, and Firefox crashes. The first patch keeps such windows
    under a new ID, the third enables the protocol on GNOME again.

    The second fixes a bug which enabling it exposed: a window restored at startup without a saved ID was never
    shown, because only MoveToWorkspace() shows a window waiting for session restore and the session store calls it
    only for a window with a saved ID. Every profile used while the protocol was disabled saves its windows without
    IDs, so the first start after enabling it showed no window at all, apart from one which requested focus.

    They need mutter with the final protocol (xdg-session-management.patch in patches/mutter/) and GTK with the
    xdg-toplevel-realized signal (patches/gtk+3.0/). The first start after switching shows every window but
    does not place it yet, since the profile has no IDs saved. From the next start on, windows return to their
    workspaces.

    Firefox's tests do not run in the package build. tests/run-session.sh checks the patches by hand, with a
    headless mutter and Firefox driven through Marionette (see tests/README): with them, restoring from the menu
    no longer crashes, a profile without IDs shows all its windows, and the next start restores each window under
    the ID it was added under. All three are under review for bug 2064100 on phabricator.services.mozilla.com, as
    D339348, D339349 and D340272. The third undoes bug 2070815, which disabled the protocol on GNOME until bug
    2064100 is fixed. Drop them once the PPA's Firefox contains the fix for bug 2064100 and no longer disables the
    protocol on GNOME, in widget/gtk/nsWindowWayland.cpp (MoveToWorkspace), widget/gtk/nsWindow.cpp
    (SessionRestoreFinished) and widget/gtk/nsAppShell.cpp (IsSessionRestoreSupported).

wayland-session-restore-uuid-ids.patch
    Restores every window under its saved ID. Before restoring a window, Firefox replaces an ID saved on X11, the
    workspace number, by a new one (Mozilla bug 2059617), and recognized such an ID by ToInteger() succeeding.
    ToInteger() also succeeds on a UUID whose first group consists of decimal digits, as about one in 43 do, so
    such a window was added to the session anew at every start and opened on the current workspace. The patch keeps
    an ID which parses as a UUID. The same code is in Firefox's main branch. Reported as Mozilla bug 2090009, under
    review as D340286 on phabricator.services.mozilla.com. Drop the patch once the PPA's Firefox contains the fix.

wayland-launch-activation-token.patch
session-restore-launch-window.patch
    Make a start of Firefox behave like a launch while it runs: the saved windows return to their places, and the
    page the launch opens appears in a new window on the current workspace, which has the focus: a URL from another
    application or, without one, the start page.

    The session store restores the first saved window into the window Firefox opens for the launch, so a URL
    becomes a tab of that window, on whichever workspace it returns to, and a launch from the dock opens nothing
    new. With session-restore-launch-window.patch, every saved window is restored into a window of its own and the
    window opened for the launch keeps the page it opened. With a URL this follows where external links open: in a
    new window when browser.link.open_newwindow.override.external is 2, otherwise the URL still becomes a tab of
    the first saved window (Mozilla bug 1565050). Without a URL it holds for a start whose settings restore the
    session (browser.startup.page set to 3), not for a restart by Firefox itself, such as after an update, or a
    recovery after a crash, which restore the windows as they were. The window opened for the launch is shown when
    session restore finishes (the second patch above), after the restored ones, so GNOME places it on the current
    workspace.

    GTK 3 activates the first window it shows with the activation token of the launch, which during session restore
    is a restored window, so with a valid token, as from the dock, GNOME switches to that window's workspace.
    wayland-launch-activation-token.patch hands the token to Firefox's own widget code instead, keeps restored
    windows from using it and drops it when every window was restored. Links opened from Ptyxis carry no token yet:
    Ptyxis opens them through libportal, which sends none to the portal.

    tests/run-session.sh checks both with every scenario, see tests/README. Neither is reported to Mozilla yet.

sigterm-handler-after-app-shell.patch
quit-at-session-end.patch
    Make Firefox quit cleanly when the desktop session ends, at logout, shutdown and reboot, so that it saves its
    session and the next start is no recovery from a crash. Such a recovery restores the windows as they were,
    without the new window session-restore-launch-window.patch opens for the launch, and loses the changes since
    Firefox last saved its session.

    Before the session ends, GNOME tells applications through the session monitor of the Inhibit portal that it
    ends, and GTK 4 applications and Chromium quit then. With the second patch Firefox creates such a monitor at
    startup, answers at once when the session asks whether it may end, and quits when it ends. A monitor does not
    keep the session from ending: patches/xdg-desktop-portal-gtk/ makes the session wait until Firefox has quit, so
    that GNOME Shell and the processes of Firefox are still there while it quits.

    Firefox 122 added a handler which quits Firefox cleanly on SIGTERM (Mozilla bug 1837907). Firefox tries to
    install it before XPCOM creates the app shell, which the handler requires, so the handler is never installed
    and SIGTERM kills Firefox at once (Mozilla bug 2086801). The first patch installs the handler once the app shell
    exists. Firefox then also quits cleanly when something else stops it: systemd at the end of a session which did
    not tell Firefox through the portal, or in which xdg-desktop-portal-gtk stopped waiting after 8 seconds, and
    systemctl or kill. The second patch quits Firefox with nsAppShell::ScheduleQuitEvent(), which the handler calls
    too. Neither patch is proposed to Mozilla yet.

    The session test in tests/session/ checks that logging out leaves sessionstore.jsonlz4 in the profile, which
    Firefox writes only when it quits cleanly, and that Firefox has quit before GNOME Shell shuts down. Drop the
    first patch once bug 2086801 is fixed, and the second once Firefox creates a session monitor itself. After a
    version bump, check that XREMain::XRE_mainRun in toolkit/xre/nsAppRunner.cpp still runs after XPCOM is
    initialized.

wayland-session-restore-no-activation.patch
    Keeps the windows restored from the session from demanding attention. On Wayland, Firefox asks the compositor
    to activate every window it shows, and session restore also focuses each window it restores, to stack the
    windows as they were. While session restore runs at startup, no event of the user is behind these requests, so
    mutter cannot activate the window and marks it as demanding attention instead. GNOME Shell then lists these
    windows first when switching windows, and Desktop Icons NG moved to the workspace of one of them at the next
    workspace switch (see patches/gnome-shell-ubuntu-extensions/). A window restored from the session now asks for
    no activation while session restore runs at startup. The window opened for the launch still receives the
    activation token of the launch.

    The requests are in nsWindowWayland::NativeShow in widget/gtk/nsWindowWayland.cpp and nsWindow::SetFocus in
    widget/gtk/nsWindow.cpp, and the patch builds on wayland-launch-activation-token.patch, which records which
    windows are restored from the session. The session test in tests/session/ checks that no restored window
    demands attention. Not proposed to Mozilla yet.

wasm-sandboxed-libraries.patch (debian-series)
    Builds graphite, ogg, hunspell, expat, woff2 and soundtouch as wasm sandboxed libraries, as Mozilla's own
    builds do, where the packaging disables them. Ubuntu 26.04's wasm32 toolchain has two bugs which block that,
    and the patch works around both with a clang resource directory in the build tree, see its description:
    Launchpad bug 2152147 (compiler-rt builtins installed where clang does not look) and Launchpad bug 2169046
    (libc++ references C++ runtime functions no library defines). Once both are fixed, only the build
    dependencies and the removed --without-wasm-sandboxed-libraries are needed. After a version bump, check that
    debian/config/mozconfig.in still disables the sandbox the same way, or the patch stops applying.

    With it, the rebuild behaves like Mozilla's build in tests/test_wasm.py, which loads each library: parsing
    XML, spell checking, decoding Ogg, loading a WOFF2 and a Graphite font, and playing audio at a changed rate.

drop-apparmor-profile.patch (debian-series)
    Stops installing /etc/apparmor.d/usr.bin.firefox. It is an enforcing profile written for Ubuntu 14.10 and
    named firefox, the same name as the profile Ubuntu's apparmor package ships in /etc/apparmor.d/firefox, which
    allows everything and grants the user namespaces Firefox's sandbox needs. The PPA's preinst disables its
    profile only on a fresh install, so switching from Mozilla's package, an upgrade for dpkg, enabled it. Under
    it Firefox crashed at startup: it could not lock its profile directory, create its Wayland proxy socket or
    run bwrap for glycin's image loaders, and GTK aborted when it failed to load an icon.

    The patch drops everything the maintainer scripts do for the profile too, so the package does not touch
    /etc/apparmor.d at all and leaves nothing there. It does not clean up after a build which installed the
    profile: such a system has to purge that build first (apt purge firefox, whose postrm removes the files),
    reload /etc/apparmor.d/firefox and then install this one. Reported as Launchpad bug 2172775. Drop the patch
    once the PPA stops shipping the profile.

Check by hand after a rebuild and a restart of Firefox:

  strings /usr/lib/firefox/libxul.so /usr/lib/firefox/liblgpllibs.so | grep -o 'rlbox_wasm2c_[a-z0-9]*' | sort -u
                                     should list expat, graphite, hunspell, ogg, soundtouch and woff2
  MOZ_LOG=WidgetWayland:4 firefox 2>&1 | grep IsSessionRestoreSupported
                                     should print nothing, as the line appears only when it is disabled
  windows on several workspaces      should return to them after the second restart
  cat /proc/$(pgrep -o -x firefox)/attr/current
                                     should print "firefox (unconfined)", Ubuntu's profile. Ubuntu's 7.0 kernel prints
                                     "unconfined": its attachment matcher mishandles the diff-encoded state the
                                     parser emits for that profile's pattern (Launchpad bug 2172229, fixed in mainline
                                     by e667fc72f7f4). "Optimize=no-diff-encode" in /etc/apparmor/parser.conf, which
                                     configure-gnome-desktop.sh adds, works around it.
  find /etc/apparmor.d -name '*firefox*'
                                     should print only /etc/apparmor.d/firefox, Ubuntu's profile
